WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.290
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons: Honor Among Thieves"
has cast a Charm spell on critics and fans

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.980
alike, and a big part of the fun is questing
after all those "DnD" Easter eggs.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.360
"I've got a good feeling about this."

00:00:12.999 --> 00:00:17.225
An important tool a Dungeon Master has at
their disposal during a campaign, especially

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:22.505
if the players are stuck or need some guidance,
is creating a non-player character -- or "NPC"

00:00:22.505 --> 00:00:23.625
-- that can help out.

00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:27.945
Special NPCs can be especially useful if the
Dungeon Master needs the players to be saved

00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:32.485
from what would otherwise be a potential "TPK"
-- or "Total Party Kill" -- encounter, where,

00:00:32.485 --> 00:00:36.825
without intervention from an NPC, all the
players' characters would end up dead.

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:42.045
Essentially, Xenk seems to be an NPC who is
plot-relevant for one specific section of

00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:46.825
the film, and leaves when his role is fulfilled
-- even if it doesn't make much sense narratively.

00:00:46.825 --> 00:00:51.335
Why does Xenk leave our heroes to battle the
world-ending threat on their lonesomes when

00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:53.895
he's obviously more skilled and capable than
them?

00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:58.314
The argument could be made that, due to Edgin's
past as a Harper, Xenk felt the quest was

00:00:58.314 --> 00:01:00.405
in good hands and he was needed elsewhere.

00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:04.925
However, it's much more fun to imagine Xenk
as a powerful NPC, controlled by the Dungeon

00:01:04.925 --> 00:01:07.239
Master of this story to move the plot along.

00:01:07.239 --> 00:01:09.445
In "Dungeons and Dragons," players use their

00:01:09.445 --> 00:01:12.264
custom characters to role-play an epic adventure.

00:01:12.264 --> 00:01:17.344
This can include fighting monsters, discovering
ancient relics, and trying to avoid deadly traps.

00:01:17.344 --> 00:01:21.585
In fact, avoiding deadly traps is a major
component of playing a standard "Dungeons

00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:23.205
and Dragons" campaign.

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:27.065
Dungeon Masters will often have fun devising
complicated and convoluted ways to navigate

00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:28.305
these various traps.

00:01:28.305 --> 00:01:33.104
The puzzles can also be overly complicated
by ambitious -- or possibly even vindictive

00:01:33.104 --> 00:01:36.125
-- Dungeon Masters who want to make their
players sweat.

00:01:36.125 --> 00:01:40.255
Towards the middle of "Dungeons and Dragons:
Honor Among Thieves," Xenk leads the protagonists

00:01:40.255 --> 00:01:45.035
into a secret underground dungeon, to find
yet another ancient magical relic.

00:01:45.035 --> 00:01:48.785
Once they reach a stone bridge, Xenk explains
that to cross the bridge, the heroes will

00:01:48.785 --> 00:01:53.485
have to do things like walking on evenly-numbered
tiles, then go back and forth to retrace their

00:01:53.485 --> 00:01:57.525
steps -- something that would require charts
and tables to complete successfully.

00:01:57.525 --> 00:02:02.785
However, all the techniques Xenk mentions
are real tricks and examples from "DnD" modules,

00:02:02.785 --> 00:02:06.885
which gives the scene a metatextual layer
of humor to those who have played with those

00:02:06.885 --> 00:02:08.381
kinds of Dungeon Masters.

00:02:08.381 --> 00:02:11.281
"I may have triggered the mechanism.

00:02:13.552 --> 00:02:15.646
So… sorry."

00:02:15.845 --> 00:02:20.135
In a classic "Dungeons and Dragons" game,
as the players explore a particular dungeon,

00:02:20.135 --> 00:02:25.225
city, or forest, they will always have a "party
order," defining who is in the front and the

00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:26.285
back of the line.

00:02:26.285 --> 00:02:30.455
This is because if an attack encounter occurs,
it helps the Dungeon Master visualize who

00:02:30.455 --> 00:02:34.825
to attack and flank initially -- though miniatures
and a board help with this as well.

00:02:34.825 --> 00:02:38.925
Now, in a regular campaign -- especially when
all the players are relatively low-level -- it's

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:44.074
a good idea to keep magic users, whether they're
sorcerers or wizards, in the back of the party

00:02:44.074 --> 00:02:46.095
so that they're safer from enemies.

00:02:46.095 --> 00:02:50.045
That's because while magic is indeed powerful,
the magic users themselves aren't very good

00:02:50.045 --> 00:02:52.595
in a fight and can't take much damage.

00:02:52.595 --> 00:02:55.535
This is why their classes are often called
"glass cannons."

00:02:55.535 --> 00:02:59.085
With this in mind, it's pretty clear that
the filmmakers behind "Honor Among Thieves"

00:02:59.085 --> 00:03:01.205
are aware of how to play the game itself.

00:03:01.205 --> 00:03:05.145
For instance, at one point -- after the heroes
are tasked with going into a secret passage

00:03:05.145 --> 00:03:09.425
to a dangerous underground ruin -- the half-elf
sorcerer Simon bluntly states,

00:03:09.425 --> 00:03:10.634
"I'll go last."

00:03:10.634 --> 00:03:14.315
It's a funny line, but it's also a nod to
the logic of the game – namely, that Simon

00:03:14.315 --> 00:03:16.994
will need to be protected if the party is
attacked.

00:03:17.689 --> 00:03:22.825
Simon Aumar has one of the most profound character
arcs of the film, starting out as a struggling

00:03:22.825 --> 00:03:27.584
magic user and becoming adept enough to help
save the world from the evil Red Wizard Sofina.

00:03:27.584 --> 00:03:31.495
The surname "Aumar" should get diehard "Dungeon
and Dragon" fans' attention.

00:03:31.495 --> 00:03:36.686
It turns out that Simon is canonically related
to Elminster Aumar, a prominent magic user

00:03:36.686 --> 00:03:39.565
in the "Forgotten Realms" campaign setting
for "DnD."

00:03:39.565 --> 00:03:43.975
In the film, Elminster is Simon's ancestor,
who shows up once Simon puts on the magical

00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:45.924
helmet and attempts to attune with it.

00:03:45.924 --> 00:03:50.355
Elminster then taunts Simon, telling him that
he isn't worthy of the Aumar name.

00:03:51.096 --> 00:03:55.555
The "Dungeons &amp; Dragons" roleplaying game
offers players a wide variety of settings,

00:03:55.555 --> 00:03:59.085
scenarios, character types, and just about
everything else a Dungeon Master and group

00:03:59.085 --> 00:04:00.584
of heroes could need.

00:04:00.584 --> 00:04:05.255
One of the most popular D&amp;D settings is "Forgotten
Realms," which features many of the creatures,

00:04:05.255 --> 00:04:09.665
locations, and notable characters that you
might think of when you picture a D&amp;D game.

00:04:09.665 --> 00:04:13.725
In "Forgotten Realms," Baldur's Gate is one
of the many major cities that DMs can set

00:04:13.725 --> 00:04:17.915
their campaign in, as well as the name of
the hugely-popular third-person isometric

00:04:17.915 --> 00:04:20.614
series of adventure games of the same name.

00:04:20.614 --> 00:04:24.285
Meanwhile, Neverwinter is another important
location in the "Forgotten Realms" setting;

00:04:24.285 --> 00:04:28.275
a large, bustling port city, it's also the
main setting for the critically-acclaimed

00:04:28.275 --> 00:04:33.185
"Neverwinter Nights" video game series, which
is yet another third-person isometric action game.

00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:37.484
The events of "Honor Among Thieves" are confirmed
to be set in the city-state of Neverwinter,

00:04:37.484 --> 00:04:41.345
and the film name-drops Baldur's Gate more
than a few times as well.

00:04:41.345 --> 00:04:45.445
Referencing both of these settings by name
gives the film an extra level of specificity…

00:04:45.445 --> 00:04:49.320
as well as delighting the old school D&amp;D fans
in the audience.

00:04:49.525 --> 00:04:54.155
In the "Dungeons and Dragons" tabletop game,
if a player rolls a 20, it's a critical success,

00:04:54.155 --> 00:04:56.834
and the player automatically succeeds at their
task.

00:04:56.834 --> 00:05:02.234
However, if they roll a 1, it's an automatic
failure, and the results are often hilarious.

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:06.106
As one fan put it on Twitter,
"Everyone expects DND to be Lord of the Rings

00:05:06.106 --> 00:05:08.984
but it always turns into Monty Python and
the Holy Grail."

00:05:08.984 --> 00:05:13.555
There are specific references to "Holy Grail"
in "Dungeons and Dragons: Honor Among Thieves,"

00:05:13.555 --> 00:05:17.035
such as Simon saying "I got better" to the
incredulous Red Witch.

00:05:17.035 --> 00:05:20.741
It can't help but remind a viewer of John
Cleese's classic line delivery.

00:05:20.741 --> 00:05:22.419
"She turned me into a newt!"

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:24.218
"A newt?"

00:05:26.345 --> 00:05:27.805
"I got better."

00:05:27.805 --> 00:05:31.924
There is also Forge's gaudy balloon that hovers
over his city throughout the film, which has

00:05:31.924 --> 00:05:36.005
a very Terry Gilliam-esque art style to it,
as do the closing credits.

00:05:36.005 --> 00:05:39.255
The main villain of the film "Dungeons &amp; Dragons:

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:42.805
Honor Among Thieves" is Sofina, the evil Red
Witch of Thay.

00:05:42.805 --> 00:05:47.535
During a flashback set before the film, we
see Sofina hire Ed, Holga, Simon, and Forge

00:05:47.535 --> 00:05:52.445
to steal an ancient artifact for her in a
secret dungeon — but Sofina ends up betraying

00:05:52.445 --> 00:05:55.505
the team by trapping Ed and Holga in a Time
Stop spell.

00:05:55.505 --> 00:05:59.275
Later, it's revealed that she was in cahoots
with Forge the whole time.

00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:03.946
In the interim, Sofina's magical advice helps
Forge amass riches and political power, while

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:08.695
she forces him to set up an event that will
get his town ready to be sacrificed in a dark

00:06:08.695 --> 00:06:11.505
ritual for her even more evil master.

00:06:11.505 --> 00:06:14.745
Turns out that master is none other than Szass
Tam.

00:06:14.745 --> 00:06:18.875
That name should get die-hard fans of "D&amp;D"
lore excited, because, in the oft-mentioned

00:06:18.875 --> 00:06:23.125
"Forgotten Realms" setting from the table-top
game, Szass is kind of a big deal.

00:06:23.125 --> 00:06:27.685
In fact, he's one of the most powerful liches
and necromancers in the game's universe, commanding

00:06:27.685 --> 00:06:30.535
a legion of undead at his beck and call.

00:06:30.535 --> 00:06:34.135
And although his plans are thwarted by our
heroes at the end of the film after they collectively

00:06:34.135 --> 00:06:38.845
defeat his servant Sofina, he is still out
there, and could possibly return for a sequel

00:06:38.845 --> 00:06:41.322
to terrorize the heroes once more.

00:06:41.322 --> 00:06:43.785
One of the most fun aspects of starting a

00:06:43.785 --> 00:06:48.060
"Dungeons &amp; Dragons" campaign is the ability
for players to create their characters from scratch.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.854
Players can choose to have elaborate and tragic
backstories, humorous and silly histories,

00:06:52.854 --> 00:06:57.095
or even dark and mysterious pasts that are
slowly revealed throughout the game.

00:06:57.095 --> 00:06:58.935
The possibilities are practically endless.

00:06:58.935 --> 00:07:03.894
Due to "DnD" being told via pen-and-paper
and good old imagination, it means that players

00:07:03.894 --> 00:07:07.345
aren't limited by much when it comes to defining
their roles.

00:07:07.345 --> 00:07:12.095
That means it's very easy for player backstories
to become overly convoluted and long-winded.

00:07:12.095 --> 00:07:15.125
This is parodied multiple times throughout
"Honor Among Thieves."

00:07:15.125 --> 00:07:19.286
One such instance finds Ed and Holga being
granted a hearing after two years stuck in

00:07:19.286 --> 00:07:21.095
a high-security prison tower.

00:07:21.095 --> 00:07:25.275
Ed gives an entire spiel about his tragic
past as a Harper leading to the death of his

00:07:25.275 --> 00:07:29.265
wife — with the fantasy creature parole
officers getting bored and annoyed in the

00:07:29.265 --> 00:07:31.805
same way other players in a real "D&amp;D" game
might.

00:07:31.805 --> 00:07:36.555
Ed even does the same thing to Xenk later
in the film when the Paladin intones, "long

00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:41.085
ago" — getting ready for a long lore dump
— until Ed angrily cuts Xenk off to get

00:07:41.085 --> 00:07:42.085
a move on.

00:07:42.085 --> 00:07:46.773
This isn't the first time that "Dungeons and
Dragons" has been adapted outside the gaming table.

00:07:46.773 --> 00:07:51.025
In the '80s, "Dungeons and Dragons" was granted
its own Saturday morning cartoon that ran

00:07:51.025 --> 00:07:55.025
on CBS for three seasons from 1983-1985.

00:07:55.025 --> 00:07:58.915
The show followed the exploits of a group
of kids who find themselves transported into

00:07:58.915 --> 00:08:04.716
the dangerous and magical world of "Dungeons
and Dragons" by a wizard with a… unique name.

00:08:04.716 --> 00:08:07.285
"I am Dungeon Master.

00:08:07.285 --> 00:08:12.415
Your guide in the realm of Dungeons &amp; Dragons."

00:08:12.415 --> 00:08:16.974
Regardless of its quality, it has its fans,
and in 2019 there was even a car commercial

00:08:16.974 --> 00:08:19.635
with live-action versions of the characters
in it.

00:08:19.635 --> 00:08:23.094
However, that wasn't the only time these characters
have been seen in live-action.

00:08:23.094 --> 00:08:26.635
Towards the climax of "Honor Among Thieves,"
the film's heroes find that there are other

00:08:26.635 --> 00:08:30.185
groups of adventurers stuck inside Forge's
dangerous maze.

00:08:30.185 --> 00:08:34.415
This includes the live-action versions of
the '80s cartoon characters – complete with

00:08:34.415 --> 00:08:38.242
the same brightly saturated costumes as their
animated counterparts.

00:08:38.745 --> 00:08:43.195
"Critical Role" is an extremely popular "Dungeons
and Dragons" podcast, and there's a subtle

00:08:43.195 --> 00:08:47.425
Easter egg in the "Dungeons and Dragons: Honor
Among Thieves" film that alludes to it.

00:08:47.425 --> 00:08:52.404
In "Critical Role," there's a fan-favorite
character named "Fresh Cut Grass," or "FCG."

00:08:52.404 --> 00:08:57.135
In the film, after escaping the botched heist,
the insecure sorcerer Simon is reintroduced

00:08:57.135 --> 00:09:01.835
barely making ends meet by performing sub-first-level
magical spells to an annoyed audience out

00:09:01.835 --> 00:09:03.745
in the middle of the countryside.

00:09:03.745 --> 00:09:08.094
This includes lighting his finger on fire
like a candlestick and creating a "fresh cut

00:09:08.094 --> 00:09:10.145
grass" scent from thin air.

00:09:10.145 --> 00:09:14.094
The spell's name is just a bit too specific
to not be nodding at one of the most popular

00:09:14.094 --> 00:09:16.517
Dungeons &amp; Dragons-related media.

00:09:16.698 --> 00:09:21.515
2023's "Dungeons and Dragons: Honor Among
Thieves" is not the first live-action film

00:09:21.515 --> 00:09:23.404
based on "Dungeons and Dragons."

00:09:23.404 --> 00:09:28.565
There was a "DnD" film that starred Jeremy
Irons, Thora Birch, and Tom Baker in 2000

00:09:28.565 --> 00:09:30.715
– one year before "Lord of the Rings."

00:09:30.715 --> 00:09:35.005
There seems to be a very subtle Easter egg
in the recent "Dungeon and Dragons" film that

00:09:35.005 --> 00:09:38.165
could be a reference to the misbegotten 2000
original.

00:09:38.165 --> 00:09:42.094
Early in the film, Ed and Holga meet up with
Forge, who ends up betraying them and trapping

00:09:42.094 --> 00:09:45.575
them in a Rug of Smothering that acts like
magical quicksand.

00:09:45.575 --> 00:09:50.115
This is similar to what led to a surprisingly
grisly death in the 2000 film.

00:09:50.115 --> 00:09:53.955
While the Rug of Smothering comes from the
original game, "Honor Among Thieves" could've

00:09:53.955 --> 00:09:58.995
used any other spell to subdue Ed and Holga
- including the Time Stop spell Sofina used

00:09:58.995 --> 00:10:00.695
on them at the beginning of the film.

00:10:00.695 --> 00:10:05.824
That seems to have been a conscious choice
by the filmmakers to pay homage to the earlier film.

00:10:05.824 --> 00:10:10.115
The Easter eggs in "Dungeons and Dragons:
Honor Among Thieves" aren't just relegated

00:10:10.115 --> 00:10:14.055
to the original games, or various ancillary
media surrounding it.

00:10:14.055 --> 00:10:16.385
There are also references to other films.

00:10:16.385 --> 00:10:21.455
This includes almost shot-for-shot homages
to the 1993 Steven Spielberg-directed dinosaur-on-the-loose

00:10:21.455 --> 00:10:24.075
sci-fi disaster film, "Jurassic Park."

00:10:24.075 --> 00:10:27.355
Two scenes in particular seem to be direct
riffs from the film.

00:10:27.355 --> 00:10:31.154
One occurs during a scene where everyone is
running from an angry red dragon, and Doric

00:10:31.154 --> 00:10:35.555
falls and hangs over a ledge as the dragon
attempts to chomp at her legs - which is framed

00:10:35.555 --> 00:10:39.995
almost exactly like the scene in "Jurassic
Park" where Lex dangles from an air-vent as

00:10:39.995 --> 00:10:43.385
a velociraptor collects itself to jump up
and attack her.

00:10:43.385 --> 00:10:48.185
Later, an arguably even more overt reference
occurs during the climax in Forge's maze.

00:10:48.185 --> 00:10:52.996
At one point, a dwarf goes to kill a panther-like
Displacer Beast...but unfortunately, the beast

00:10:52.996 --> 00:10:57.735
casts an illusion of itself with one of its
magical back-tendrils, and the real beast

00:10:57.735 --> 00:10:59.645
pounces on the old dwarf.

00:10:59.645 --> 00:11:03.575
It's a short similar to the classic Jurassic
Park scene where Robert Muldoon gets outsmarted

00:11:03.575 --> 00:11:05.859
by two raptors in "Jurassic Park."

00:11:08.935 --> 00:11:10.514
"Clever girl."

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:17.935
Speaking of that red dragon, "D&amp;D" die-hards
might recognize the powerfully pudgy beast.

00:11:17.935 --> 00:11:21.615
Named Themberchaud, this character from the
"D&amp;D" tabletop role-playing game is known

00:11:21.615 --> 00:11:26.404
to stalk The Underdark area of Gracklstugh,
AKA the "City of Blades."

00:11:26.404 --> 00:11:31.165
According to "Out of the Abyss," Themberchaud
trades his treasure-protecting services for

00:11:31.165 --> 00:11:33.165
the life of a pampered dragon.

00:11:33.165 --> 00:11:37.474
In lore, Themberchaud is intelligent, formidable,
and a different kind of force to be reckoned

00:11:37.474 --> 00:11:41.255
with than his simpler — and cuter — presentation
in "Honor Among Thieves."

00:11:41.255 --> 00:11:42.485
This was intentional.

00:11:42.485 --> 00:11:45.654
The film's co-writer and director John Francis
Daley told Polygon:

00:11:45.654 --> 00:11:49.575
"We really leaned into the fact that he's
not like any other dragon you've seen before

00:11:49.575 --> 00:11:50.575
on film.

00:11:50.575 --> 00:11:53.865
And what was so cool is that it doesn't feel
like we're betraying the source material by

00:11:53.865 --> 00:11:57.815
having a dragon so uniquely different, because
D&amp;D itself is so unique."

00:11:58.221 --> 00:12:03.865
Simon is a wobbly young sorcerer suffering
from a seriously limiting bout of imposter

00:12:03.865 --> 00:12:06.024
syndrome in "Honor Among Thieves."

00:12:06.024 --> 00:12:10.085
As gratifying as it is to watch him grow more
confident and firmer on his feet over the

00:12:10.085 --> 00:12:14.985
course of the movie, there's a name that never
stops scaring him — Mordenkainen, of Arcane

00:12:14.985 --> 00:12:17.355
Seal of Mordenkainen fame.

00:12:17.355 --> 00:12:21.205
Mordenkainen's unbreakable seal protects the
vault our party needs to bust into in order

00:12:21.205 --> 00:12:23.334
to nab the Tablet of Reawakening.

00:12:23.334 --> 00:12:28.594
In fact, if there's an Arcane Seal named after
you in the world of "D&amp;D," you're probably

00:12:28.594 --> 00:12:31.084
a total baddie, just like Mordenkainen.

00:12:31.084 --> 00:12:36.305
In tabletop game lore, Mordenkainen is a masterful
archmage responsible for creating countless

00:12:36.305 --> 00:12:37.394
powerful spells.

00:12:37.394 --> 00:12:42.274
To be the stuff of legend in a legendary game
is no easy feat, which makes sense considering

00:12:42.274 --> 00:12:47.935
Gary Gygax created Mordenkainen as a player
character in one of the earliest-ever "D&amp;D"

00:12:47.935 --> 00:12:51.445
campaigns, according to this archived Q&amp;A
with Gygax.

00:12:51.579 --> 00:12:56.534
In "Honor Among Thieves," The Harpers are
a secret society of good guys working as spies

00:12:56.534 --> 00:12:57.915
to protect the Realms.

00:12:57.915 --> 00:13:01.555
Edgin signs up with The Harpers for their
do-good brigade in an attempt to lend his

00:13:01.555 --> 00:13:03.454
Bard skills to keeping the peace.

00:13:03.454 --> 00:13:07.695
Edgin even carries around their giant little
book of oaths and pledges until he sours on

00:13:07.695 --> 00:13:11.935
the peanuts being a good guy tends to pay
— and steals some unfortunately marked Red

00:13:11.935 --> 00:13:13.065
Wizard gold.

00:13:13.065 --> 00:13:17.545
Ed's theft not only sets him apart from The
Harpers, but it ultimately costs him his wife,

00:13:17.545 --> 00:13:20.645
his home, and almost destroys his relationship
with his daughter.

00:13:20.645 --> 00:13:24.565
Ed's feelings about his actions are complicated
and more than a little shameful throughout

00:13:24.565 --> 00:13:25.565
the movie.

00:13:25.565 --> 00:13:30.154
Still, Xenk, a far more lawful good guy than
Ed, encourages him to take up the mantle of

00:13:30.154 --> 00:13:31.735
Harper once more.

00:13:31.735 --> 00:13:35.925
Perhaps Xenk is inspired to keep Ed in the
Harper game because of the sentiment shared

00:13:35.925 --> 00:13:40.245
in the Harper chant, recorded in Ed Greenwood's
1993 "The Code of the Harpers":

00:13:40.245 --> 00:13:41.875
"We are the Harpers.

00:13:41.875 --> 00:13:46.654
We are the Lord Protectors of the Realms,
Fools, all―but the Gods look down and smile

00:13:46.654 --> 00:13:47.995
glory upon us.

00:13:47.995 --> 00:13:50.825
Weep for us, watch for us, and hope in us.

00:13:50.825 --> 00:13:52.901
We shall not fail thee."

00:13:52.901 --> 00:13:57.635
If you're someone who loves "Honor Among Thieves"
but aren't super versed in "D&amp;D" lore, Sophia

00:13:57.635 --> 00:14:03.654
Lillis' druid character Doric might seem the
most fantastic of the entire ragtag gang.

00:14:03.654 --> 00:14:09.405
Doric's a tiefling druid, gifted with "wild
shape" shapeshifting ability, has small horns,

00:14:09.405 --> 00:14:13.875
pointed ears, and is also a proud member of
a society known as the Emerald Enclave.

00:14:13.875 --> 00:14:18.255
Viewers who are up on their "D&amp;D" lore, however,
know that the Emerald Enclave are the sworn

00:14:18.255 --> 00:14:22.875
protectors of nature, a druidic order that
cares for the green children of the Realms.

00:14:22.875 --> 00:14:27.474
While the society has its own circles of rule
and gods not explored in "Honor Among Thieves,"

00:14:27.474 --> 00:14:32.255
the focus on protecting nature is shared both
onscreen and in the tabletop RPG world.

00:14:32.255 --> 00:14:36.885
In the movie, Doric looks out a window at
swathes of land destroyed by necromantic magic

00:14:36.885 --> 00:14:41.375
and urges her party to protect the land while
there is still something to protect.

00:14:41.375 --> 00:14:45.575
Just like in real life, the environmentally-focused
Emerald Enclave struggle for their concerns

00:14:45.575 --> 00:14:50.834
to be taken seriously by selfish spellcasters
and crooked politicians alike, as shown both

00:14:50.834 --> 00:14:55.797
in "Honor Among Thieves" and the tabletop
"Dungeons and Dragons" Forgotten Realms campaigns.

00:14:56.088 --> 00:14:59.945
One of the joys of "Honor Among Thieves" is
that it's entertaining whether you have an

00:14:59.945 --> 00:15:05.115
encyclopedic knowledge of "D&amp;D" lore or are
simply a fan of good times and Chris Pine.

00:15:05.115 --> 00:15:09.284
The movie is accessible and enjoyable to any
level of viewer — but moviegoers on the

00:15:09.284 --> 00:15:11.834
hunt for Easter eggs won't be disappointed.

00:15:11.834 --> 00:15:16.865
One such example is the "D&amp;D" version of profanity,
uttered by a shocked soldier after Simon's

00:15:16.865 --> 00:15:18.094
image spell goes sideways.

00:15:18.094 --> 00:15:20.415
But what are the "Nine Hells" anyway?

00:15:20.415 --> 00:15:23.895
"The Nine Hells" are the "D&amp;D" world's version
of Hell.

00:15:23.895 --> 00:15:26.594
The Nine Hells of Baator boasts nine layers.

00:15:26.594 --> 00:15:29.855
Each layer, or plane, has its own ruler and
its own flavor.

00:15:29.855 --> 00:15:34.815
Fire, ice, astral hell, and bogs — there
is truly a layer of hell for everyone in The

00:15:34.815 --> 00:15:36.215
Nine Hells.

00:15:36.215 --> 00:15:39.865
Tabletop players can take the highway to hell
by playing the "Dungeons and Dragons" adventure,

00:15:39.865 --> 00:15:41.812
"Descent Into Avernus".

00:15:42.295 --> 00:15:47.565
To some, Revel's End is the blink-and-you'll-miss-it
name of the tower prison Edgin and Holga escape

00:15:47.565 --> 00:15:50.315
from using the power of birdman Jarnathan.

00:15:50.315 --> 00:15:55.764
To others, it is the panopticon standing sentry
in Icewind Dale, carved from the Windbreak,

00:15:55.764 --> 00:15:58.935
overlooking the Sea of Ice, heated by magical
continual flames.

00:15:58.935 --> 00:16:02.945
Revel's End is used to bookend the movie "Dungeons
and Dragons: Honor Among Thieves."

00:16:02.945 --> 00:16:07.185
The film opens with the aforementioned dramatic
window-leaping escape and closes with the

00:16:07.185 --> 00:16:11.634
crooked Forge Fitzwilliam attempting to jump
to his freedom the same way, only to crack

00:16:11.634 --> 00:16:15.884
right into a window, bricked up by an Absolution
Council that is slowly getting hip to its

00:16:15.884 --> 00:16:17.284
prisoners' ways.

00:16:17.284 --> 00:16:21.735
In the "D&amp;D" tabletop games, Revel's End appears
first in the prison-break adventure "Icewind

00:16:21.735 --> 00:16:23.484
Dale: Rime of the Frostmaiden."

00:16:23.484 --> 00:16:27.585
The adventure was developed by Wizards of
the Coast to support the world the movie makers

00:16:27.585 --> 00:16:28.585
were developing.

00:16:28.585 --> 00:16:33.025
The book came out in 2020 while the movie
came out in 2023, which makes Revel's End

00:16:33.025 --> 00:16:36.715
a sort of infinity loop Easter egg in both
properties.

00:16:36.715 --> 00:16:40.755
The snowy stronghold is even harder to escape
in these sessions, considered challenging

00:16:40.755 --> 00:16:43.025
to even experienced Dungeon Masters.

00:16:43.025 --> 00:16:48.205
"Rime of the Frostmaiden" features a dragon,
terrifying ice monsters, and a sperm whale.

00:16:48.205 --> 00:16:53.475
And to us at least, getting to see Chris Pine
ride one of those would have been quite the spectacle.

00:16:53.475 --> 00:16:57.795
One man's trash is another man's treasure,
but in "Honor Among Thieves" one man's treasure

00:16:57.795 --> 00:17:01.744
could also be a chest full of razor-sharp
teeth and a snake-like tongue determined to

00:17:01.744 --> 00:17:03.815
sap the life force of anyone nearby.

00:17:03.815 --> 00:17:07.905
The treasure chest that Holga and the gang
encounter in Forge's arena isn't a treasure

00:17:07.905 --> 00:17:11.765
chest at all — it's an old "D&amp;D" villain
called a "Mimic."

00:17:11.765 --> 00:17:16.065
The Mimic first shows up in "Dungeons and
Dragons" lore in the original Monster Manual,

00:17:16.065 --> 00:17:21.655
published by Gary Gygax in 1977, and has appeared
in Monster Manuals ever since.

00:17:21.655 --> 00:17:26.625
Like quite a few early "D&amp;D" villains, Mimics
use shape-shifting and ooey-gooeyiness to

00:17:26.625 --> 00:17:29.415
stun, deceive, and attack unsuspecting parties.

00:17:29.415 --> 00:17:33.364
In tabletop campaigns, Mimics get players
that get too close into a sticky situation

00:17:33.364 --> 00:17:34.484
— literally.

00:17:34.484 --> 00:17:38.604
Hitting or kicking a Mimic only makes it bond
more tightly to a player, which makes us happy

00:17:38.604 --> 00:17:42.135
Holga didn't try to land a punch on one in
the movie.

00:17:42.135 --> 00:17:46.575
The Gelatinous Cube is a classic "D&amp;D" creature
that's part all-consuming monster and part

00:17:46.575 --> 00:17:47.845
JELL-O Jiggler.

00:17:47.845 --> 00:17:53.015
These monsters engulf unlucky players and
deal out relentless acid damage to those unlucky

00:17:53.015 --> 00:17:55.685
enough to get up close and personal with The
Gelatinous Cube.

00:17:55.685 --> 00:18:00.295
The disgusting danger of The Gelatinous Cube
makes its use in a key "Dungeons and Dragons:

00:18:00.295 --> 00:18:03.095
Honor Among Thieves" escape scene all the
more delicious.

00:18:03.095 --> 00:18:07.665
Our heroes dive into the cube to escape Forge's
games — and the Displacer Beast that's hot

00:18:07.665 --> 00:18:08.665
on their tails.

00:18:08.665 --> 00:18:13.095
Due to Doric's Wild Shape ability, she's able
to wiggle out of trouble and yank the rest

00:18:13.095 --> 00:18:14.355
of the gang out.

00:18:14.355 --> 00:18:19.595
The Gelatinous Cube is an old "D&amp;D" creature,
invented by "D&amp;D" co-creator Gary Gygax along

00:18:19.595 --> 00:18:24.245
with many other canonical characters for the
legendary Greyhawk campaign – but it's had

00:18:24.245 --> 00:18:26.195
some brushes with movie stardom before.

00:18:26.195 --> 00:18:31.225
Notably, The Gelatinous Cube plays a role
in a movie saturated with "D&amp;D" imagery — Pixar'

00:18:31.225 --> 00:18:32.225
"Onward."

00:18:34.007 --> 00:18:36.153
"A gelatinous cube!"

00:18:36.325 --> 00:18:42.405
Xenk leads our favorite band of ragtag thieves
into The Underdark to find the Helmet of Disjunction,

00:18:42.405 --> 00:18:46.435
and even saying the name of the place seems
to put a chill in Xenk's voice.

00:18:46.435 --> 00:18:50.935
If a subterranean environment sends a shiver
up the spine of a business-like and battle-ready

00:18:50.935 --> 00:18:55.565
semi-eternal paladin, it beyond terrifies
the rest of the "Dungeon and Dragons: Honor

00:18:55.565 --> 00:18:56.885
Among Thieves" gang.

00:18:56.885 --> 00:19:01.755
But hey — a quest wouldn't be a quest without
at least one lair our heroes might never escape.

00:19:01.755 --> 00:19:05.775
Of course, The Underdark is also a setting
for "Dungeons and Dragons" campaigns in the

00:19:05.775 --> 00:19:07.515
world of the tabletop RPG.

00:19:07.515 --> 00:19:12.665
It's a massive, layered network of darkened,
demon-stuffed caverns and other hidey-holes

00:19:12.665 --> 00:19:14.725
that feature in many campaigns.

00:19:14.725 --> 00:19:18.104
One of the creatures they encounter in the
film — and an easter egg itself — is the

00:19:18.104 --> 00:19:19.775
Intellect Devourer.

00:19:19.775 --> 00:19:24.715
These hideous little scamps boast brain-shaped
bodies on webbed feet, and despite their looks

00:19:24.715 --> 00:19:27.315
are fearsome living death merchants.

00:19:27.315 --> 00:19:32.305
The Intellect Devourers show up as true forces
to reckon with in "Underdark" campaigns in

00:19:32.305 --> 00:19:34.395
the "Dungeons and Dragons" tabletop games.

00:19:34.395 --> 00:19:38.675
In "Honor Among Thieves," Xenk warns the party
that the approaching Intellect Devourers are

00:19:38.675 --> 00:19:41.970
more likely to attack prey with high intelligence.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:43.660
"Well that's a little hurtful."

00:19:43.822 --> 00:19:48.535
Filmmakers Jonathan Francis Daley and Jonathan
Goldstein have stated in multiple interviews

00:19:48.535 --> 00:19:52.935
that they were influenced by fantasy comedy
classic "The Princess Bride" when writing

00:19:52.935 --> 00:19:54.645
"Dungeons and Dragons: Honor Among Thieves."

00:19:54.645 --> 00:19:57.824
Now that's a true genre-bending…
"…marriage."

00:19:57.824 --> 00:20:02.735
But we also see clear references to "Ocean's
Eleven" and other classic heist films.

00:20:02.735 --> 00:20:06.775
"Honor Among Thieves" uses the visual language
of crime capers to set up the plan of the

00:20:06.775 --> 00:20:11.395
great vault heist and then launch us right
into the middle of it not going to plan.

00:20:11.395 --> 00:20:15.915
There's also a classic "D&amp;D" tabletop reference
in the heist portrait the party uses to cover

00:20:15.915 --> 00:20:18.905
Simon's Hither Thither staff-created portal.

00:20:18.905 --> 00:20:24.456
The man portrayed in this portrait is Volothamp
"Volo" Geddarm, recognizable to "D&amp;D" players

00:20:24.456 --> 00:20:28.114
for his in-world guidebooks to the realms
of "Dungeons and Dragons."

00:20:28.114 --> 00:20:33.045
Volo rates the various inns and locations
he frequents with a pipe system and his opinions

00:20:33.045 --> 00:20:34.975
can be sought out in many guidebooks.

00:20:34.975 --> 00:20:39.374
If Danny Ocean ever had to put a team together
to infiltrate the Forgotten Realms, we bet

00:20:39.374 --> 00:20:40.863
he'd call on Volo.

00:20:41.144 --> 00:20:46.635
Who knew a hulking beast that is part bloodthirsty
bear and part giant owl could be so cute?

00:20:46.635 --> 00:20:50.744
Move over, "Cocaine Bear" – there's a new
cinematic bear in town, based on a classic

00:20:50.744 --> 00:20:53.215
"D&amp;D" creature: The Owlbear.

00:20:53.215 --> 00:20:57.004
Viewers new to the world of "D&amp;D" will see
The Owlbear in "Dungeons &amp; Dragons: Honor

00:20:57.004 --> 00:21:01.385
Among Thieves" for what it is — a powerful
beast of ultimate cuteness and destruction.

00:21:01.385 --> 00:21:04.965
Longtime tabletop RPG players might have a
bone to pick, however.

00:21:04.965 --> 00:21:09.545
In the movie, Doric is a Tiefling druid who
can transform into The Owlbear as part of

00:21:09.545 --> 00:21:11.155
her Wild Shape power.

00:21:11.155 --> 00:21:15.185
In the world of tabletop "Dungeons and Dragons,"
this transformation wouldn't be possible,

00:21:15.185 --> 00:21:19.455
because The Owlbear is classified as a "monstrosity"
and not a "beast."

00:21:19.455 --> 00:21:23.104
Classification has a different impact on the
games than on the screen.

00:21:23.104 --> 00:21:27.645
Still, some tie-in prequel novels at least
attempt to lay the groundwork for the "impossible"

00:21:27.645 --> 00:21:32.325
transformation, which should calm even those
with extremely Lawful Evil alignments.

